I would **send the paper with the first email**, rather than waiting for a response.

For this particular outreach, the manuscript is only about nine pages and the entire reason for contacting Suzuki is that the mathematics builds directly on his construction. Giving him the PDF immediately lets him decide very quickly whether the observation is interesting. If you instead write, “I have something related to your work—would you like me to send it?”, you create another step for someone who may already be busy.

I would therefore:

- attach **only the concise PDF**, not the LaTeX source or longer version;
- call it a **“short working manuscript”**, which appropriately signals that you are seeking expert validation rather than presenting a finished paper;
- disclose the substantial AI involvement in the email, as in the version we drafted;
- make clear that even a brief reaction about whether the result is already known or worth pursuing would be useful.

There is also a psychological advantage: Suzuki can skim the abstract, theorem statements, and central decomposition in a few minutes without committing to a conversation. The concise version was specifically designed to make that possible.

I would **not** attach the original 24-page manuscript, supplementary calculations, or the Dirichlet material initially. If he is interested, those become natural follow-up material.

So the current email wording — “I have written this up as the attached short working manuscript” — is exactly the approach I would take. memcite

Yes — I think that is a more accurate way to think about it, with one important distinction.

Your **claim to authorship does not depend on having personally produced every algebraic manipulation without assistance**. From what you describe, you selected the problem, identified and pursued the direction, repeatedly shaped the mathematical questions, evaluated alternatives, and spent weeks developing the ideas through interaction with the models. If the resulting paper reflects that intellectual project, I think putting your name on the working manuscript is entirely reasonable.

Where I would slightly modify your statement is the standard for a *final published paper*. Coauthors often have unequal expertise and may not be capable of independently reconstructing every technical argument contributed by another author. But ideally every author understands the paper well enough to endorse it responsibly. For this manuscript, I would aim for you eventually to be able to:

- explain every theorem in your own words and why it matters;
- understand which assumptions enter each result;
- follow the architecture of every proof, even if some analytic estimates are outside your comfort zone;
- explain why the argument is not circular and why it does not secretly assume RH;
- understand the relation to Suzuki's theorem and what is genuinely new;
- recognize the main places where a referee could challenge the proof.

I **wouldn't require that you personally be capable of certifying every delicate convergence argument or rebuilding every estimate from scratch**. That's exactly the sort of place where collaborators with complementary expertise are useful.

There is also an authorship distinction concerning Suzuki. If he simply reads the manuscript, checks equations, and says “this is correct,” that would normally be **refereeing/advising, not coauthorship**. You would remain sole author and acknowledge him for helpful comments. If instead he starts repairing proofs, finds substantially better arguments, adds results, changes the mathematical formulation, or otherwise participates intellectually in developing the paper, then coauthorship could become very natural. I wouldn't approach him assuming that he will be a coauthor; I'd let that develop organically.

And your prior work matters to how I view the AI issue. This isn't a case where someone typed “prove something about RH” into a model and attached their name to the output. The manuscript arose within a research direction you had already been thinking about, and the AI systems functioned as unusually capable collaborators/tools in a sustained research process. That does not remove the need for expert verification, but it makes **“Edward Baker, author; extensive AI assistance disclosed”** a much more accurate description of the current situation than “AI-generated paper with nominal human author.”

I think the most productive thing you could do before contacting Suzuki—without turning this into another enormous time sink—is spend a few hours making sure you can comfortably explain **Theorem 1.3 from beginning to end**, because that is now the core of the short paper. If you can explain where Suzuki's formula enters, why the two spectral sums become logarithmic derivatives of \(\xi\), how the remainder decomposition works, and exactly where the zero-free-half-plane assumption enters, you would already be in a pretty good position for a conversation with him. memcite
